<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xue Rengui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/614">614</a>–<a href="/years/683">683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xue Rengui was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> general who rose to fame in Emperor Taizong's campaign against Goguryeo in 645 and later served in the Tang conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. In 670 he led a large Tang army against the Tibetan Empire but was routed at the Dafei River near Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a> by the Tibetan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-trinring-tsendro">Gar Trinring Tsendro</a>. Recalled to service in old age, he defeated the Eastern Turks in 682.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xue-rengui.jpg" alt="Xue Rengui" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ma Tai (馬駘; 1885-1937) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A9%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%89%E7%AE%AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xue Rengui, whose formal name was Xue Li, was born in 614 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a>. Little of his early life was recorded except that he was said to be a poor farmer and that his wife was surnamed Liu. In 644, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Emperor Taizong of Tang</a> prepared to attack Goguryeo, Lady Liu urged him to seek honours in the army before reburying his ancestors, and he volunteered under the general Zhang Shigui.</p><p>At the front he rescued the general Liu Jun'ang from encircling Goguryeo troops, killed their commander and hung his head on his saddle, which won him promotion. Before the city of Ansi in 645 he charged the relief army of Ko Yŏnsu and Ko Hyejin in white armour with a ji and two bows. The Goguryeo army broke, and Taizong, who had asked who the man in white was, rewarded him with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and a command in the imperial guards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Emperor Gaozong he climbed the gate of Wannian Palace during a flash flood in 654 to warn the emperor, who escaped as the water reached his bedchamber. In 657 he advised releasing the captive family of the Nishu chief, who then joined the Tang against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-helu">Ashina Helu</a>, and in 659 he defeated the Goguryeo general Wen Shamen and captured the Qidan chief Abugu, for which he was made Baron of Hedong.</p><p>Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> coalition in 661 he killed three champions with three arrows, but after their surrender had the ten and several thousand others killed; he was arrested for this and released when Gaozong judged his achievements greater than his crimes. In 668 he took Buyeo and some 40 cities before joining Li Ji at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>. After Goguryeo fell he was made protector general there and Duke of Pingyang, and was said to have won the allegiance of its people for a while.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat of 670 he was arrested, released and stripped of his posts, and probably in 675 he was exiled to Xiang Prefecture for reasons the records do not make clear. Recalled in 681, he died in March 683, and his sons Xue Ne and Xue Chuyu also served as generals. His life was dramatised in the Yuan play Xue Rengui's Glorious Return Home by Zhang Guobin and the Qing novel Xue Rengui's Campaign to the East; folklore makes him father of the fictional general Xue Dingshan, and he appears as a door god in Chinese and Taoist temples and as a general god in Korean shamanism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xue Rengui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the minister Gar Tongtsen and his sons, Tibet conquered the Tuyuhun around Lake Kokonor in 663, and in 670 the general Gar Trinring Tsendro defeated a Tang army under <b>Xue Rengui</b> at the Dafei River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
